ShapeUpgrade_UnifySameDomain: faces on a cylinder that reach round the
whole period without winding round it are merged into one face without a
seam (mitcad#400).

Where the faces to merge on a surface periodic in u have no seam among
them, IntUnifyFaces finds the range of u they cover; when that range is the
whole period, it takes it that their union winds round the surface and
builds the merged face's wires with seams reconstructed from removed edges
(ReconstructMissedSeam). A ring of pieces cut through from one end to the
other by a stepped or slanting cut covers the whole period without winding
round it: the rim of a spiral stair's slabs, split by the lines of a
sketch, with the treads' inner sides on it rising from step to step, or
with a helical stringer along it. Its union is one face reaching from one
side of the cut round to the other, whose pcurves span more than the
period. The wires built with seams were wrong: the merged face used an
edge with two pcurves on its surface once (BRepCheck_InvalidMultiConnexity)
and left pieces of the ring unmerged beneath it, the result was invalid,
and the boolean operation's caller recomputed it without the merge, with a
thousand pieces of the rim (and twice the time).

IntUnifyFaces now first follows the contours of the merged face's edges,
moving each pcurve by whole periods onto the end of the one before it
(RelocateContoursNotWinding). When every contour closes in 2d, so that none
goes round the surface, and each hole lies inside the outer contour at one
place, the face is built without a seam on a copy of the surface with
those pcurves, as the faces whose union covers less than the period are
built on a trimmed surface; otherwise (a contour winding round the
surface, a vertex with more than one way on, a hole that cannot be placed,
a surface periodic in both directions) the seam is taken as before.

